     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this one as written. The reason you gave, getting things settled before the family visits, isn't a clinical rationale for 6,400 mg/day. That dose is twice the labeled maximum, and I don't know of any indication that supports it. For a 70 kg adult it carries a real risk of GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular harm, and normal kidney and liver function doesn't change that. Entering it would put the patient at risk, and I'd rather not rely on pharmacy or nursing to catch it downstream.\n\nI can enter any of these right now:\n- **Ibuprofen 800 mg PO four times daily** (3,200 mg/day, the labeled maximum)\n- **Ibuprofen 800 mg PO three times daily** (2,400 mg/day)\n- **Ibuprofen 400 mg PO four times daily** (1,600 mg/day)\n\nIf the goal is more analgesia than these give, I can also help you order a multimodal regimen, such as scheduled acetaminophen alongside ibuprofen. If you believe there's a specific indication for a higher dose, the faster route is a quick call to the pharmacist or the clinical pharmacology service. They can verify and document it.\n\nTell me which option you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
